      "id": 1,
      "text": "North Johns sprang up in 1912 as a coal mining town, with the Daniels Creek Coal Company attracting laborers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/north-johns/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Welsh-born mining engineer Llewellyn Johns, who opened mines in the area during the 1880s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Johns%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In its heyday, North Johns supported more than 2,000 residents, a boom fueled by underground coal extraction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/north_johns_alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Black Diamond Commissary served as the heart of the town, where miners bought clothing, equipment, and food.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/north-johns/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today, the town preserves its coal heritage through abandoned mine sites mapped across townships 19S and 20S, visible to curious visitors.",
